{
  "annotator_prompt": [
    {
      "name": "target_locations",
      "description": "During container exchanges, identities were usually followed correctly but different choices of silhouette point and projection height produced locations differing by more than 1 cm. One exchange also contains an inconsistent annotation: a green-associated cover was annotated on the upper branch of a crossing, although continuity into the subsequent settled arrangement requires the lower branch. These are PREDICTABILITY failures. Define each ordered entry as the world [x,y] of the requested cover's central vertical axis, initially that of its uncovered cube. Localize a resting cover using the center of its horizontal top face at approximately z=0.06 m, not its whole silhouette centroid or a table-plane projection of its top. Preserve the existing coordinate when the cover has not moved; update after observed translation, carrying the last reliable estimate through occlusion. Use millimetre-resolution values without implying millimetre measurement accuracy. Audit exchanges continuously across the whole episode, correcting the first erroneous association and its subsequent values rather than switching identities at the settled endpoint. In the inconsistent green-target exchange, the rightmost target follows the right-hand moving cover into the lower of the two vertically separated central covers, and subsequently into the middle settled cover. Keep requested pickup order fixed. Example value: [[0.017, -0.017]].",
      "value_type": "world_coordinate"
    },
    {
      "name": "tracking_frame",
      "description": "During a container approach, the arm partially obscured the target in the front view while its center remained visible in the current wrist view. The annotation selected the current frame but the front-only rule required an older frame, breaking PREDICTABILITY. Define this field as the latest shown frame label at which all still-needed targets can be localized using the available front image and, when available for that label, wrist image together. Partial occlusion is permissible if the target's center and correspondence remain unambiguous. Update when such a frame is shown; otherwise retain the preceding label. Initialize with E0. Example value: \"E265\"."
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [
    {
      "name": "target_locations",
      "description": "During shuffling, correct target identities were sometimes assigned inaccurate coordinates because the projection landmark was unspecified; at a crossing, following a spatial rank instead of the continuous path also assigned the wrong cover. These failures break PREDICTABILITY and can later break SUFFICIENCY for pickup. Store an ordered list of requested targets' central-axis world [x,y] coordinates. For a resting cover, intersect the calibrated ray through its horizontal top-face center with approximately z=0.06 m; do not use its silhouette centroid or intersect its elevated top with the table plane. Initialize from the visible requested cubes and associate covers as they descend. Update coordinates when chronological frames show translation; preserve carried coordinates when the target is stationary rather than introducing estimation jitter. During crossings, follow each cover's continuous trajectory through every intermediate frame, allowing its vertical and horizontal image ranking to change. In particular, a right-hand moving cover may become the lower of a vertically aligned pair; do not transfer its identity to the upper cover merely because that cover is closer to its previous position. Use the retained frame to establish initial correspondence, and carry the last reliable location through occlusion. Keep tracking later requested targets during the earlier pickup. Example value: [[0.017, -0.017]]."
    },
    {
      "name": "tracking_frame",
      "description": "A target remained localizable in the current wrist image despite front-camera arm occlusion, but the front-only wording selected an unnecessarily old frame and disagreed with the annotation, breaking PREDICTABILITY. Write the latest shown label for which all still-needed targets can be localized unambiguously from the available front and wrist views together. The current wrist image may qualify its shared current-frame label; do not assume a wrist image is available for every intermediate motion frame. Partial front occlusion alone does not disqualify a label if the available images still establish the target center and correspondence. Otherwise retain the incoming label. Initialize with E0. Example value: \"E265\"."
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_action": "When pressing both buttons before the requested pickups, the policy repeatedly treated the achieved hover TCP position as the button center. Long approaches had lateral endpoint errors exceeding 1 cm, so both the subsequent closing waypoint and press inherited the error. Transfers to the other button also used an imprecise projection reference. This breaks SUFFICIENCY of the action instructions, not the memory schema: the current calibrated images contain the required alignment information. Replace the ambiguous 'same center' interpretation with an explicit rule applying to both buttons: independently localize the center of the circular press surface, using its approximately 0.025 m surface height for calibrated ray intersection, rather than the square base centroid, table-plane projection, or the other button's x coordinate. Prefer the current calibrated wrist view when the circle is visible. At hover and ready milestones, recompute that center and command its x,y, even if the preceding approach was accepted as complete and even if the separate closing action produced little TCP motion. Hover tolerance establishes the milestone, not exact alignment. Close over that visually localized center at z=0.15, then press over it at commanded z=0.0075; retain the existing recognition of contact near z=0.024 and retract over the button center to z=0.15. Apply the nonidentical-waypoint check only after correcting x,y: add about 3 mm vertically only when the corrected nominal close/open waypoint is still within 2 mm of the current TCP. Do not add that offset when lateral correction already makes the waypoint nonidentical. Keep the separate right-button closing milestone, existing gripper commands, and container manipulation rules. No additional remembered button coordinates or episode-specific coordinates are needed.",
  "refused": "The crossing report that proposed explicitly assigning the green-associated cover to the upper of two central covers is not passed on. The later report correctly identifies an inconsistency between that incoming association and the annotated middle-cover destination, but those two reports cannot both be adopted. Reviewing the continuous exchange shows that the right-hand moving target follows the lower branch into the middle settled position. The correction is to the earlier association and its propagated coordinates, not an instruction to switch identities later or infer hidden color from the final arrangement. Also, preserving a stationary coordinate is not a ban on updating a cover that actually moves during grasping or release; only unsupported visual-estimation jitter should be suppressed."
}

optimizer.text:
{
  "annotator_prompt": [
    {
      "kind": "modify",
      "field": "target_locations",
      "edits": [
        {
          "modify_from": "Ordered list of world [x,y] locations of the containers associated with the cube colors requested in the instruction, in requested pickup order. Before covering, use the corresponding visible cube locations. At covering, associate each cube with the container descending over it. Subsequently follow that container continuously through every shuffle; its initial table slot is not its identity. Update to the last observed location each cycle, retaining a location through temporary arm occlusion.",
          "modify_to": "Ordered list of central-axis world [x,y] coordinates of the containers associated with the requested cube colors, in fixed requested pickup order. Before covering, use the central axes of the corresponding visible cubes. At covering, associate each cube with the container descending over it. Localize a resting container using its horizontal top-face center projected at approximately z=0.06 m, not its silhouette centroid or a table-plane projection of its elevated top. For a lifted container, account for its observed elevation. Follow each container continuously through every shuffle, allowing its horizontal and vertical image ranking to change at crossings; neither its initial slot nor nearest position alone establishes identity. Check correspondence through successive frames across the episode and correct an erroneous association at its first occurrence and throughout its subsequent values, rather than switching identities at the endpoint. Update coordinates after observed translation, including movement during grasping or release. Preserve the carried coordinate when stationary and retain the last reliable estimate through temporary occlusion. Write coordinates to millimetre resolution without implying millimetre measurement accuracy."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "modify",
      "field": "tracking_frame",
      "edits": [
        {
          "modify_from": "Label of the latest shown front frame in which all still-needed target containers, or their initially uncovered cubes, can be localized without arm occlusion. Retain the preceding value if no such frame appears in the cycle. This image anchors correspondence between the stored target locations and the next motion clip. Initialize with E0.",
          "modify_to": "Latest shown frame label at which all still-needed target containers, or their initially uncovered cubes, can be localized unambiguously using the available front image and, when available for that label, wrist image together. Partial occlusion is permissible when the target centers and correspondence remain unambiguous. Do not assume wrist images exist for intermediate motion frames. Retain the preceding value if no qualifying frame appears in the cycle. This keyframe anchors correspondence between the stored target locations and the next motion clip. Initialize with E0."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [
    {
      "kind": "modify",
      "field": "target_locations",
      "edits": [
        {
          "modify_from": "Read the requested colors in instruction order and initialize an ordered list of their visible cube world [x,y] locations using calibration. Match each descending cover to the cube underneath. Using the carried locations and tracking_frame, follow those specific containers through the cycle's chronological frames and write their latest [x,y] locations. Follow motion through exchanges rather than relabeling by nearest initial slot or final left-to-right order. Keep tracking a later requested target during button pressing and the earlier pickup. Preserve the last reliable location during temporary occlusion; use a newly exposed cube to confirm the association.",
          "modify_to": "Read the requested colors in instruction order and initialize an ordered list of their visible cubes' central-axis world [x,y] coordinates using calibration. Keep this requested order fixed. Match each descending cover to the cube underneath. For a resting cover, project its horizontal top-face center onto approximately z=0.06 m; do not use its silhouette centroid or project its elevated top onto the table plane. For a lifted cover, account for its observed elevation. Using the carried locations and tracking_frame to establish initial correspondence, follow each specific container through the cycle's chronological frames. At crossings, use continuous motion through intermediate frames, allowing horizontal and vertical image ranking to change; do not transfer identity by nearest previous position, initial slot, or final spatial order alone. Update coordinates after observed translation, including movement during grasping or release. Preserve stationary coordinates rather than introducing estimation jitter, and carry the last reliable location through temporary occlusion. Keep tracking later requested targets during button pressing and earlier pickups; use a newly exposed cube to confirm correspondence. Write coordinates to millimetre resolution without implying millimetre measurement accuracy."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "modify",
      "field": "tracking_frame",
      "edits": [
        {
          "modify_from": "Write the label of the latest frame shown this cycle in which all still-needed target containers, or initially uncovered target cubes, can be localized without arm occlusion. If none qualifies, carry the previous label. At the first cycle write E0. Use this retained image with target_locations to establish identities at the beginning of the next clip.",
          "modify_to": "Write the latest shown label for which all still-needed target containers, or initially uncovered target cubes, can be localized unambiguously from the available front and wrist views together. The current wrist image may qualify its shared current-frame label; do not assume a wrist image is available for every intermediate motion frame. Partial occlusion is permissible if the available images establish the target centers and correspondence unambiguously. If none qualifies, carry the previous label. At the first cycle write E0. Use this retained keyframe with target_locations to establish identities at the beginning of the next clip."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_action": [
    {
      "modify_from": "Button rules: From start, approach the robot-left button center at z=0.15 m, orientation [-3.1416,0,0], gripper open.",
      "modify_to": "Button rules: Independently localize each button using the center of its circular press surface, intersecting its calibrated image ray with approximately z=0.025 m. Do not use the square base centroid, a table-plane projection, the other button's x coordinate, or the achieved TCP position as its center. Prefer the current calibrated wrist view when the circle is visible. At hover and ready milestones, recompute the button center and command its x,y even if the preceding approach was accepted as complete or the closing action produced little motion. Hover tolerance establishes the milestone, not exact lateral alignment. All references below to the button center mean this visually localized center. From start, approach the robot-left button center at z=0.15 m, orientation [-3.1416,0,0], gripper open."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "At left_pressed, retract to z=0.15 closed.",
      "modify_to": "At left_pressed, retract over the localized left-button center to z=0.15 closed."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "At right_pressed, retract to z=0.15 closed.",
      "modify_to": "At right_pressed, retract over the localized right-button center to z=0.15 closed."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "Never issue an exactly identical TCP pose merely to change the gripper. If the nominal close/open waypoint is within 2 mm of the current pose, add a small vertical displacement of about 3 mm while retaining the required gripper command.",
      "modify_to": "Never issue an exactly identical TCP pose merely to change the gripper. Apply this check only after correcting the nominal close/open waypoint's x,y from visual localization. If the corrected nominal waypoint is still within 2 mm of the current pose, add a small vertical displacement of about 3 mm while retaining the required gripper command. Do not add that offset when lateral correction already makes the waypoint nonidentical."
    }
  ],
  "refused": "Do not encode the episode-specific green-target branch or middle-slot destination, including the rejected upper-branch assignment. Continuous trajectory correspondence, with correction of erroneous annotations from their first occurrence, covers crossings without memorizing a particular arrangement or inferring hidden color from an endpoint. No new button-coordinate memory or episode-specific coordinates are added."
}
